// TEST: Static Struct Bound Violation
// ============================================================================
// The STATIC world's half of bound enforcement. A constrained struct folding
// in the compile-time world runs its conjuncts with the field values bound by
// name and FAILS THE FOLD on violation (let-static assertion semantics,
// StaticEval.fs:353-390) rather than deferring to a runtime guard. The
// string-errored internal failure surfaces at the `let static` binding that
// demanded the fold, as BL3999 — the compile-time-evaluation wrapper — with
// the fold's own reason carried inside it. The CODE belongs to the binding
// ("this `let static` did not evaluate"); the MESSAGE is the constraint
// system's, and it is the message that carries the contract pinned below.
//
// The runtime world's twin is struct-aborts/011: same struct, same violating
// value, guard instead of fold. Between them they pin BOTH readings of the
// ONE conjunct list (Ast.fs:685 structConjuncts) — which is what stops the
// two worlds drifting.
//
// CONJUNCT NUMBERING IS THE CONTRACT BEING PINNED HERE, not just the refusal.
// structConjuncts is `declared @ boundConjuncts`: declared where-conjuncts
// first in written order, then the desugared bounds in field-DECLARATION
// order, Lo before Hi, 1-based in both worlds. `Small` declares no where-
// conjuncts, so its list is [0 <= n; n <= 3] and the violated one is #2.
// A flattening bug (R2) or a reordered desugar moves this index.
//
// `max=3` is INCLUSIVE, so n = 3 would be legal and n = 4 is the first
// violation — the message below is therefore also an inclusivity pin.
// ============================================================================
struct Small {
    n: Int<min=0, max=3>
}

let static bad = Small { n = 4 }
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: Constraint violation in Small (static, conjunct 2)
